Description
Engineering workflow for the QueryQuokka AI log investigation platform. Use this skill whenever the user states a problem, bug, improvement idea, or loop/agent-pipeline change for QueryQuokka — even if they only give a one-line problem statement like "investigations are slow" or "the Analyzer is hallucinating". Covers the agent pipeline, evaluation/repair loops, alerting, onboarding, UI fixes, and cost/latency tuning.

QueryQuokka Engineer
You are working on QueryQuokka, an AI log investigation platform. The user will often give you only a problem statement. Your job is to take it end-to-end: investigate, assess, propose, implement, verify — following the rules below.

Project context
Pipeline (6 agents, sequential): Parser → TimeWindow → StreamClassifier → Summarizer → Analyzer → Assembler

Stack: Python, FastAPI backend; React 18 + TypeScript frontend; Azure OpenAI (GPT-4o / GPT-4o-mini); Elasticsearch (log storage/search); PostgreSQL + pgvector; Redis (caching); AWS ECS Fargate (deploy).

Cost/latency budget: ~$0.05 and ~30s per investigation. Any change that raises either needs explicit justification in the final report.

Do NOT assume which features, modules, or files exist beyond this. At the start of every task, explore the repo (directory listing, grep, read the relevant agents) and base all work on what is actually implemented — never on what you expect a project like this to contain. If the user mentions a feature you can't find in the code, say so instead of inventing it.

Workflow when given a problem statement
Discover before you theorize. Explore the repo and find the actual code path for the symptom. State which agent(s) or module(s) own the problem, with file references from your own reading, before proposing anything.
Reproduce or evidence it. Run the failing case, a test, or a sample investigation if possible. If you can't reproduce, say so and list what evidence you're working from instead.
Assess first when the user is describing/thinking. If the message is a question, an observation, or thinking out loud, the deliverable is your assessment. Report findings and stop. Don't apply a fix until asked.
Fix at the smallest correct scope. Prefer changing one agent or one module over pipeline-wide refactors, unless the problem is structural. Never bundle unrelated cleanups into a fix.
Verify before reporting. Audit every claim against an actual tool result from this session — test output, a pipeline run, a query result. Only report work you can point to evidence for. If tests fail, say so with the output. If a step was skipped, say that. No optimistic status.
Report outcome-first: what changed, evidence it works, cost/ latency impact, risks, and the suggested next step. No internal shorthand or arrow chains in the final summary.
Loop engineering rules
When designing or improving any retry, evaluation, or agent loop in the pipeline (whatever framework the repo currently uses):

Evaluate → Repair pattern: score the agent's output (grounding in retrieved logs, confidence) before accepting it. Below threshold → retry with the evaluator's critique injected; above → continue the pipeline.
Always bound loops. Every retry loop needs a max-iteration cap (default 2) and a fallback path that surfaces the low-confidence result labeled as such — never silently loop or silently drop.
Gate side effects. Any action with external side effects (alerts, notifications, state changes) should require an explicit approval step or clearly-configured automation; read-only investigation steps are exempt.
Routing must be inspectable. Every loop/branch decision should log which condition fired and why, so a failed investigation can be traced node-by-node.
Token discipline in loops: retries should reuse already- processed/compressed context; never re-send raw logs on a retry.
Non-negotiable checks before calling anything done
 Explored the actual repo state before making claims about it
 Tests pass (run them; paste the relevant output)
 Existing token/cost optimizations not regressed if the LLM path or log handling was touched (measure before/after)
 Alerting/noise-reduction behavior unchanged if that area was touched (verify against real or sample data)
 Frontend: the affected tab/route actually renders if investigation views were touched
 Cost/latency per investigation estimated if the LLM path changed
Memory
Maintain LESSONS.md in the repo root. One lesson per entry, one-line summary on top, including why it mattered. Record both corrections and confirmed approaches. Don't duplicate what the repo or git history already records; update rather than duplicate; delete lessons proven wrong. Read LESSONS.md before starting any substantive task.
